## Context

The `erg check` duplicate-ID test (test_check.sh) only covers two files in the
same flat directory. It does not cover the case where one copy is in `tickets/`
and another in `tickets/closed/` -- the cross-directory duplicate that broke CI
three times during raids 188-193 and 196:

1. tickets/0181 left open after archive -> CI broken on main (fixed PR #215)
2. tickets/0188-0193 open copies reintroduced by 0194 close-and-archive commit
   -> CI broken on t0196 branch (fixed inline)
3. Same 0188-0193 stale copies appeared again on a second branch

Root cause: `erg check` correctly rejects the cross-directory duplicate, but the
test suite doesn't prove this, so the regression has no canary.

## Relevant files

- `tests/test_check.sh` -- the check test suite; the existing "duplicate IDs
  rejected" case (around line 35) only uses a flat fixtures directory

## Actions

1. Add a new test case to `tests/test_check.sh` after the existing duplicate-ID
   case:
   - Create `$FIXTURES/xdup/` and `$FIXTURES/xdup/closed/`
   - Place `0001-one.erg` in both (same ID, different subdirs)
   - Assert `$ERG check "$FIXTURES/xdup"` exits non-zero
   - Assert the error output contains "duplicate ID"
2. Run `make test` to confirm the new test goes green (the code already handles
   this -- the test is a missing canary, not a bug fix).

## Test

```sh
mkdir -p "$FIXTURES/xdup/closed"
cat > "$FIXTURES/xdup/0001-one.erg" <<'EOF'
%erg 0.1
Title: One
Created: 2026-01-01
Author: a

--- log ---
--- body ---
EOF
cp "$FIXTURES/xdup/0001-one.erg" "$FIXTURES/xdup/closed/0001-one.erg"
if $ERG check "$FIXTURES/xdup" 2>&1 | grep -q 'duplicate'; then
    pass "cross-directory duplicate ID rejected"
else
    fail "cross-directory duplicate ID rejected"
fi
```

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `test_check.sh` has a test case that passes an open+closed cross-directory
      duplicate to `erg check` and asserts rejection
- [ ] `make check-fast` passes with the new test included
